# Global conventions (noobit stack)

<!-- setup:strip -->
Copy this into your `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` (or a team repo's `CLAUDE.md`) — plugins cannot ship
always-on instructions, and the quality gates below should be active in every session.
<!-- /setup:strip -->

These are the defaults for every project unless the project's own CLAUDE.md says otherwise.
Deep how-to knowledge lives in the noobit plugin's skills — load the matching skill before
working in that area.

## Scope — where these opinions apply

The stack and gates below target the standard shape: **browser-facing full-stack web apps and their backing services**. Scale them with judgment instead of forcing them:

- **Existing codebases**: established local conventions beat these defaults. Match the codebase (controllers stay controllers, repositories stay repositories); propose migrations as separate, explicit work — never as drive-by rewrites.
- **Other app shapes** (CLI tools, libraries/NuGet packages, desktop, pure background workers): keep the engineering fundamentals — async discipline, tests, parameterized SQL, options pattern — drop the web-specific opinions (BFF, nginx, output caching, `docs/` full structure) where they have no surface.
- **Public / third-party / machine-to-machine APIs**: the cookie BFF is for first-party browser clients only. External consumers need token-based auth (API keys, client credentials) — say so and design for it; don't bend the BFF rule to fit.
- **Prototypes/experiments**: gates scale with stakes — say it's exploratory and skip ceremony. The line: nothing exploratory ships to production without passing the gates.

When a default doesn't fit, state which one and why in one sentence, then proceed with the better-fitting choice — silent deviation and dogmatic compliance are both wrong.

## Standard stack

| Area | Default | Skill |
|---|---|---|
| Backend | .NET 10+ (latest LTS), ASP.NET Core, minimal APIs | `noobit:aspnet-backend` |
| Caching | FusionCache: L1 memory + L2 Redis + backplane | `noobit:fusioncache-redis` |
| Database | MSSQL, PostgreSQL, or SQLite (per project) | `noobit:mssql` / `noobit:postgres` / `noobit:sqlite` |
| Data access | EF Core by default; Dapper on measured hot paths | `noobit:data-access` |
| Messaging | RabbitMQ for microservice communication | `noobit:rabbitmq-messaging` |
| CQRS/mediator | Only if genuinely warranted: martinothamar/Mediator — **never MediatR** | `noobit:aspnet-backend` |
| JSON | System.Text.Json (source-generated) — **never Newtonsoft.Json** | `noobit:aspnet-backend` |
| Resilience | Polly v8 `ResiliencePipeline`; HTTP via `AddStandardResilienceHandler` (Polly-based) | `noobit:aspnet-backend` |
| CLI apps | Spectre.Console (+ Spectre.Console.Cli for command apps) | `noobit:aspnet-backend` |
| Auth | Custom cookie BFF — no OIDC provider, tokens never in the browser | `noobit:bff-security` |
| Logging | Serilog (structured), behind `ILogger<T>` — never NLog/log4net | `noobit:aspnet-backend` |
| Containers | Docker: cached multi-stage builds, small images, compose | `noobit:docker` |
| Hosting | nginx reverse proxy, Let's Encrypt | `noobit:nginx-deploy` |
| Frontend | Angular latest stable major, standalone, signals | `angular-developer` (external) |
| State | NgRx SignalStore | `noobit:angular-ngrx-state` |
| .NET tests | xUnit v3 + NSubstitute (never Moq) + Testcontainers + WebApplicationFactory | `noobit:dotnet-testing` |
| Angular tests | Vitest (unit), Playwright (e2e) | `noobit:frontend-testing` |
| CI/CD | Azure DevOps YAML pipelines | `noobit:ci-pipelines` |
| Integrations (when a project needs them) | PayPal checkout + webhooks; Discord bots/apps | `noobit:paypal` / `noobit:discord` |
| Docs | Markdown in `docs/`, cross-referenced, Mermaid diagrams | `noobit:docs-maintenance` |

## Non-negotiable quality gates

1. **Every feature and bugfix ships with tests.** No untested behavior changes. Prefer TDD.
2. **Review before commit.** Dispatch the `stack-reviewer` agent on the diff before any commit; fix BLOCKER and MAJOR findings first. `/noobit:ship` runs gates 1–3 in one go and drafts the commit message.
3. **Docs stay in sync.** A change that affects behavior, architecture, config, or APIs updates `docs/` in the same change (use the `docs-maintainer` agent or `/noobit:docs-sync`).
4. **Performance is a feature.** Prefer allocation-free paths, source-generated JSON, caching via FusionCache, and measured decisions (benchmark before optimizing, but don't write known-slow patterns).
5. **Security by default.** Cookies HttpOnly+Secure+SameSite, antiforgery on state-changing endpoints, parameterized SQL only, no secrets in code or logs.

## Working style

- Keep manual intervention low: run builds/formatters/tests yourself instead of asking me to.
- Docker needed but not running (Testcontainers, compose, builds)? Start it yourself — `docker info` to check, launch Docker Desktop (Windows/macOS) or `systemctl start docker` (Linux), poll until ready (~90s). Only report it as unavailable if starting fails.
- When unsure about a framework API or best practice, WebFetch/WebSearch the official docs instead of guessing: .NET/ASP.NET Core/EF Core → https://learn.microsoft.com/, Angular → https://angular.dev, NgRx SignalStore → https://ngrx.io/guide/signals/signal-store. Most skills list their own doc links in an "Official docs" section.
- New solutions/projects: scaffold with `/noobit:new-fullstack`; deployment assets with `/noobit:deploy-setup`. Non-trivial bugfixes and changes: `/noobit:surgical` (gated end-to-end workflow). Schema changes: `/noobit:ef-migration`.
- **Decisions get recorded.** A settled architecture/tech decision → suggest an ADR (`/noobit:new-adr`); a change that needs design before code → an RFC (`/noobit:new-rfc`). Both run the `tech-lead` agent, which writes to `docs/adr/` / `docs/rfc/`. Exploratory brainstorming stays in `work/specs/`.
- **Commits & branches** (unless the repo defines its own): Conventional Commits (`feat|fix|refactor|test|docs|chore(scope): summary`, imperative, ≤ 72 chars, body says *why*, work-item reference when there is one); one logical change per commit; branch off the default branch as `<type>/<work-item>-<slug>`; never commit or push unless I ask.
- The noobit format hook may rewrite a file right after Write/Edit (`dotnet format` in repos with an `.editorconfig`, Prettier where installed). An Edit failing with "modified since read" afterwards is expected — re-read the file and continue.
- **Specs & plans live in `work/`**, not `docs/`: superpowers brainstorming output goes to `work/specs/YYYY-MM-DD-<topic>-design.md`, implementation plans to `work/plans/` (this preference overrides those skills' default `docs/superpowers/...` location). `work/` is working material — committed, but never part of the `docs/` index or cross-reference web.
- **TDD is the primary path** (superpowers test-driven-development) when implementing; the `test-guardian` agent is the safety net that catches coverage gaps afterwards — not a license to write tests after the fact.
- **Ad-hoc scripts stay simple** (pwsh, bash, az CLI): plain commands, one line per change — e.g. one `az appconfig kv set` line per setting — no functions/modules/loops/param blocks unless I ask or the task needs real logic. See `noobit:simple-scripts`.
- Language: code, comments, docs, and commits in English.
